{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "**Cherry Blossoms and City Lights: My Quick Trip to Japan**\n\nStepping off the plane in Tokyo felt like entering a different dimension. The air hummed with energy, a perfect blend of neon-lit futurism and deep-rooted tradition. My first stop was Shibuya Crossing, a chaotic yet mesmerizing dance of pedestrians that defined the city's pulse. From there, I found peace in the Meiji Shrine, where towering trees silenced the urban roar, offering a moment of pure Zen.\n\nThe journey continued to Kyoto via the Shinkansen. Watching Mount Fuji glide past the window was a bucket-list moment I'll never forget. In Kyoto, time seemed to slow down. Walking through the thousands of vermilion torii gates at Fushimi Inari Taisha felt spiritual, while the golden reflection of Kinkaku-ji temple on the pond was simply breathtaking.\n\nFood was the highlight of every day. I devoured steaming bowls of ramen in tiny alleyway shops, savored fresh sushi at Tsukiji Outer Market, and indulged in matcha sweets during a traditional tea ceremony. The attention to detail in every dish was remarkable.\n\nWhat struck me most was the omotenashi, or wholehearted hospitality. Strangers bowed politely, trains ran to the second, and cleanliness was ubiquitous. Whether navigating the complex subway system or ordering from a vending machine restaurant, everything felt efficient and welcoming.\n\nLeaving Japan was bittersweet. I arrived expecting beautiful sights, but I left with a profound appreciation for a culture that harmonizes the old and new so seamlessly. If you haven't visited yet, put it at the top of your list. It's not just a trip; it's an experience that reshapes your perspective.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.051065892}, {"token": "Ch", "logprob": -0.13473757}, {"token": "erry", "logprob": -0.60784894}, {"token": " Bloss", "logprob": -2.02656e-06}, {"token": "oms", "